FerrionOS change to the Redox kernel (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit e7f1d4231d0f4b55fbef229d9cd9459302c101f4 with ferrion-quiet-boot.patch and ferrion-boot-name.patch applied (security roadmap S13, lockdown):
- the memory scheme has two roots: the one the kernel hands the boot process opens physical memory, memory of another type or physically contiguous, address translation and the new port-io handle; the restricted root (opened as `restricted-root`) allocates ordinary write-back memory only, and `scheme-root` reopens the root it is asked through;
- a call on a port-io handle lets the calling thread use I/O ports (x86 and x86_64);
- the Iopl verb on a thread's own handle now fails with EPERM, so I/O ports come only through a port-io handle;
- an open the restricted root refuses logs the caller's pid and the path.

